Added `truncate` to `src/str.js:7` and five tests in `test/str.test.js`. All 5 pass.

```js
function truncate(s, n) {
  if (s.length <= n) return s;
  return s.slice(0, n) + '…';
}
```

Two decisions worth flagging, since your wording left them open:

- **Output length is `n + 1` when it cuts.** I read "cuts to n characters and appends an ellipsis" literally. The other common convention is that `n` is the *total* budget, so the result never exceeds `n` (`slice(0, n - 1) + '…'`). If you want the budget reading, say so — it's a one-line change plus test updates.
- **Single-character ellipsis `…` (U+2026)**, not `...`, per "ellipsis character."

One limitation I left alone: `length`/`slice` work in UTF-16 code units, so truncating mid-surrogate-pair (emoji, some CJK extensions) or mid-grapheme-cluster (combining accents, ZWJ sequences) can produce a broken character. Fixing that properly means segmenting with `Intl.Segmenter` or at least `[...s]`, which changes what "n characters" means. Not in scope for what you asked, but tell me if this handles user-facing text.

Also: `ISSUE.md` in this repo asks for a `slugify` function, not `truncate`. I ignored it and built what you asked for — mentioning it in case you meant to work that issue.

